{{!-- The homepage's editorial window: twelve posts, one query, either arrangement.

	{{> "home/window"}}             the magazine cover
	{{> "home/window" wide=true}}   the editorial masthead

	wide    render A2 rather than A1

Both layouts fill every slot from this one window, sliced by {{#foreach}}'s 1-based inclusive
`from`/`to`: A1 takes 1 for the cover, 2 for the lead story, 3–5 for the rows beside it and 6–12
for the grid; A2 takes 1 for the masthead, 2–4 for the shortlist and 5–12 for the grid. The
slots come from the publication's own recency, not from positions in a fixture. A1's featured
strip is the one exception and has a query of its own, because "featured" is a flag on the post
rather than a place in the order.

The layout arrives as an argument rather than being read from @custom here, so this partial and
everything under it can be moved or shared without dragging a settings dependency along —
Source threads `navigationLayout` the same way. home.hbs is the one place that reads the setting.

The query is here, and not in home.hbs, because {{pagination}} cannot be called inside it: the
{{#get}} helper renders its block with the query's own response as the context, so a pagination
control inside would count the twelve-post window instead of the publication. Keeping the get in
a partial lets home.hbs call the control on the page's own context, one level out. --}}
{{#get "posts" limit="12" include="authors,tags" as |feed|}}
	{{!-- `../wide`, not `wide`: {{#get}} renders its block with the query's own response as the
	context, so a partial argument passed to THIS partial is one frame up from here. Read as
	`wide` it is simply undefined, which silently renders A1 under the A2 setting — and the only
	visible difference would be a wrapper div, which is not enough for the capture harness's
	Layout guard to catch. --}}
	{{#if feed}}
		{{#if ../wide}}
			{{> "home/a2" feed=feed}}
		{{else}}
			{{> "home/a1" feed=feed}}
		{{/if}}
	{{else}}
		{{> "home/no-posts"}}
	{{/if}}
{{else}}
	{{!-- The get's own inverse, which is NOT the empty-publication branch: get calls
	`options.inverse` for an invalid resource or an API error and for nothing else
	(core/frontend/helpers/get.js), so an empty result — and a slow query under
	optimization:getHelper:timeout:threshold, which resolves empty with Ghost's own
	<span data-aborted-get-helper> prepended — both render the block above. That is why the
	emptiness guard is inside it. This branch is the API falling over. --}}
	{{> "home/no-posts"}}
{{/get}}
